1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,590
Can artificial intelligence
be conscious? Not intelligent.

2
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:07,190
We already know it can be intelligent.

3
00:00:08,159 --> 00:00:09,849
I mean something much stranger.

4
00:00:10,859 --> 00:00:13,289
Can AI actually experience something?

5
00:00:13,779 --> 00:00:16,529
Can it have a subjective
experience of the world?

6
00:00:17,304 --> 00:00:20,224
Can there be something inside
the system that experiences the

7
00:00:20,304 --> 00:00:25,503
world and somehow knows that
it is experiencing it: "I am."

8
00:00:27,491 --> 00:00:29,521
At first, the answer seems obvious.

9
00:00:30,331 --> 00:00:31,921
AI is just mathematics.

10
00:00:33,031 --> 00:00:35,081
Algorithms, parameters, statistics.

11
00:00:36,124 --> 00:00:39,074
But then... What exactly is
our consciousness made of?

12
00:00:41,434 --> 00:00:44,364
That turns out to be a
surprisingly difficult question.

13
00:00:45,234 --> 00:00:46,644
We know that we are conscious.

14
00:00:47,574 --> 00:00:50,104
Right now, you know that
you are watching this video.

15
00:00:50,899 --> 00:00:53,849
You see the images. You hear the sound.

16
00:00:54,659 --> 00:00:56,769
You may be thinking
about what I am saying.

17
00:00:57,639 --> 00:01:00,529
And most importantly, you
have a subjective experience.

18
00:01:01,497 --> 00:01:05,887
If you cut your finger, you don't merely
know that some tissue has been damaged.

19
00:01:06,841 --> 00:01:08,041
It hurts.

20
00:01:09,764 --> 00:01:12,084
When you see the colour
red, your eyes aren't

21
00:01:12,164 --> 00:01:14,794
simply detecting a particular
wavelength of light.

22
00:01:15,641 --> 00:01:17,751
You actually experience seeing red.

23
00:01:17,901 --> 00:01:22,471
And this subjective experience is one
of the biggest mysteries in science.

24
00:01:23,419 --> 00:01:25,159
Because we still don't
really know how physical

25
00:01:25,239 --> 00:01:28,809
processes in the brain
produce subjective experience.

26
00:01:31,469 --> 00:01:34,659
You might expect consciousness
to have a specific location.

27
00:01:35,609 --> 00:01:37,809
Something like: "Here's vision."

28
00:01:37,889 --> 00:01:39,739
"Here's memory."

29
00:01:40,409 --> 00:01:42,199
"And somewhere in the middle,
here's consciousness."

30
00:01:43,958 --> 00:01:45,928
But it doesn't seem to be that simple.

31
00:01:46,758 --> 00:01:49,048
There are several competing
theories of consciousness,

32
00:01:49,618 --> 00:01:52,508
and scientists still don't
agree on which one is correct.

33
00:01:53,379 --> 00:01:56,179
But much of modern consciousness
research focuses on

34
00:01:56,259 --> 00:01:59,199
how different parts of the
brain communicate, and how

35
00:01:59,279 --> 00:02:02,729
information becomes integrated
across large-scale networks.

36
00:02:03,672 --> 00:02:07,102
So perhaps the right question
isn't: "Where is consciousness?"

37
00:02:08,248 --> 00:02:12,118
Maybe the better question is: "What
is the system doing as a whole?"

38
00:02:14,308 --> 00:02:18,558
Imagine an orchestra. A single
violin isn't a symphony.

39
00:02:19,168 --> 00:02:20,878
Neither is a single trumpet.

40
00:02:21,875 --> 00:02:25,555
But when all the instruments interact
in a particular way, something

41
00:02:25,635 --> 00:02:29,365
emerges that none of the individual
instruments has on its own.

42
00:02:30,056 --> 00:02:33,166
Some theories of consciousness
propose something similar.

43
00:02:34,016 --> 00:02:37,026
Consciousness may not be a
single component of the brain.

44
00:02:37,898 --> 00:02:39,198
It may be a property of a particular

45
00:02:39,278 --> 00:02:42,228
organisation of many
interacting components.

46
00:02:42,518 --> 00:02:45,848
And we have some fascinating
evidence for this.

47
00:02:48,148 --> 00:02:50,418
We can actually turn consciousness off.

48
00:02:51,288 --> 00:02:52,848
When a doctor puts you under general

49
00:02:52,928 --> 00:02:55,918
anaesthesia, your brain
doesn't simply stop.

50
00:02:56,673 --> 00:02:59,003
You're not dead. Your
brain is still functioning.

51
00:02:59,253 --> 00:03:02,063
But your conscious experience disappears.

52
00:03:03,173 --> 00:03:06,103
And when the anaesthesia wears
off, consciousness returns.

53
00:03:07,077 --> 00:03:11,657
Research on anaesthesia shows that
losing consciousness is associated with

54
00:03:11,737 --> 00:03:17,107
major changes in the communication and
dynamics of large-scale brain networks.

55
00:03:17,989 --> 00:03:20,049
Because it suggests
that it's not enough for

56
00:03:20,129 --> 00:03:23,159
individual parts of the
brain to simply be working.

57
00:03:23,909 --> 00:03:27,539
What matters is also how they are
connected and how they interact.

58
00:03:28,429 --> 00:03:30,299
And now we can come back to AI.

59
00:03:33,219 --> 00:03:35,689
This is where things
get really interesting.

60
00:03:36,679 --> 00:03:38,819
If consciousness is
somehow a property of a

61
00:03:38,899 --> 00:03:41,729
particular organisation
of a physical system...

62
00:03:42,511 --> 00:03:44,781
why would that system
have to be biological?

63
00:03:45,651 --> 00:03:47,581
Why would it have to be made of neurons?

64
00:03:48,604 --> 00:03:51,014
Why couldn't it be made
of electronic circuits?

65
00:03:51,824 --> 00:03:54,234
Or some completely different
physical substrate?

66
00:03:55,166 --> 00:03:57,266
Of course, this does not
mean that a sufficiently

67
00:03:57,346 --> 00:04:00,736
large computer will
automatically become conscious.

68
00:04:01,555 --> 00:04:03,745
We have no idea whether that would happen.

69
00:04:03,855 --> 00:04:06,795
But we also don't have a
good reason to assume that

70
00:04:06,875 --> 00:04:10,265
consciousness is physically
impossible outside biology.

71
00:04:12,985 --> 00:04:15,075
And now we run into a huge problem.

72
00:04:15,985 --> 00:04:18,385
Suppose we ask an AI: "Are you in pain?"

73
00:04:18,465 --> 00:04:22,955
And it answers: "Yes. I feel
pain." Is it conscious? Maybe.

74
00:04:23,816 --> 00:04:25,796
But it could also simply be generating a

75
00:04:25,876 --> 00:04:28,846
sentence that is appropriate
to the conversation.

76
00:04:29,779 --> 00:04:31,749
So we ask: "Are you afraid of death?"

77
00:04:32,339 --> 00:04:33,399
And it says: "Yes.

78
00:04:33,479 --> 00:04:37,129
I don't want to stop existing."
Even more convincing.

79
00:04:38,135 --> 00:04:39,955
But we still don't know
whether there is actually

80
00:04:40,035 --> 00:04:42,965
anything being experienced
inside the system.

81
00:04:43,935 --> 00:04:47,445
And the better AI becomes,
the harder this problem gets.

82
00:04:48,302 --> 00:04:51,802
Because a system could potentially
perfectly imitate the outward behaviour

83
00:04:51,882 --> 00:04:56,992
associated with consciousness without
having any subjective experience at all.

84
00:04:59,702 --> 00:05:01,952
And here's an even stranger possibility.

85
00:05:03,022 --> 00:05:06,052
What if we eventually create
a genuinely conscious AI...

86
00:05:06,942 --> 00:05:09,552
and it tells us: "No. I'm not conscious."

87
00:05:10,497 --> 00:05:11,477
How would we know it's lying?

88
00:05:11,557 --> 00:05:15,047
Or suppose it says: "Yes. I am conscious."

89
00:05:15,937 --> 00:05:17,607
How would we know that isn't a lie?

90
00:05:18,362 --> 00:05:19,982
We don't currently have
a machine that we can

91
00:05:20,062 --> 00:05:23,752
connect to an AI and
see: Consciousness: yes.

92
00:05:24,623 --> 00:05:27,903
Researchers are therefore trying
to identify observable indicators

93
00:05:27,983 --> 00:05:31,253
of consciousness based on
different scientific theories.

94
00:05:31,463 --> 00:05:34,273
But this is still an
active area of research.

95
00:05:36,773 --> 00:05:38,903
Someone will certainly
say: "That's nonsense.

96
00:05:39,173 --> 00:05:42,573
AI is just mathematics." Okay.

97
00:05:42,653 --> 00:05:45,083
But then we can ask: And what are humans?

98
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:47,710
Our brains are made of neurons.

99
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:50,950
Neurons exchange electrical signals.

100
00:05:51,660 --> 00:05:53,510
They release neurotransmitters.

101
00:05:54,168 --> 00:05:56,258
Their electrical potentials change.

102
00:05:57,148 --> 00:06:00,738
Chemical reactions take
place. All of this is physics.

103
00:06:01,756 --> 00:06:05,566
We can of course say: "Humans
are conscious. Computers aren't."

104
00:06:05,896 --> 00:06:08,126
That's a perfectly legitimate position.

105
00:06:09,107 --> 00:06:12,617
But then we have to explain:
What exactly is the difference?

106
00:06:13,467 --> 00:06:17,737
What does a biological system have that
no artificial system could ever have?

107
00:06:20,247 --> 00:06:24,657
One possibility is that consciousness
isn't something we put into a system.

108
00:06:25,442 --> 00:06:27,742
Maybe it appears when a
system becomes sufficiently

109
00:06:27,822 --> 00:06:32,012
complex and its components
interact in a particular way.

110
00:06:32,749 --> 00:06:36,819
Think about water. A single
water molecule isn't wet.

111
00:06:37,689 --> 00:06:40,349
Huge numbers of molecules
interacting together produce

112
00:06:40,429 --> 00:06:45,799
properties that individual molecules
don't have. We call this emergence.

113
00:06:46,875 --> 00:06:49,545
Maybe consciousness
works in a similar way.

114
00:06:50,299 --> 00:06:52,649
If that's actually how
consciousness works...

115
00:06:52,839 --> 00:06:57,449
then it isn't obvious that biological
matter is a necessary requirement.

116
00:07:00,239 --> 00:07:01,989
Now we need to slow down.

117
00:07:02,799 --> 00:07:05,969
Because none of this means that
today's language models are conscious.

118
00:07:06,929 --> 00:07:09,039
Consider a large language model.

119
00:07:09,669 --> 00:07:11,229
Behind what looks like an intelligent

120
00:07:11,309 --> 00:07:14,639
conversation are enormous
mathematical models.

121
00:07:15,561 --> 00:07:17,271
The model receives an input.

122
00:07:17,941 --> 00:07:19,971
It converts the input into tokens.

123
00:07:20,949 --> 00:07:24,389
Through many layers of
neural-network computation, it builds

124
00:07:24,469 --> 00:07:27,859
representations of relationships
between those tokens.

125
00:07:28,789 --> 00:07:32,419
And eventually it calculates
probabilities for what should come next.

126
00:07:33,269 --> 00:07:36,979
Then it does it again.
And again. And again.

127
00:07:37,745 --> 00:07:41,625
The result can be several pages
of explanation, a program,

128
00:07:41,705 --> 00:07:45,275
a translation, or a surprisingly
sophisticated argument.

129
00:07:46,182 --> 00:07:48,902
And yet there doesn't have to be
a little person inside the machine

130
00:07:48,982 --> 00:07:53,532
reading the text and thinking: "Okay,
now I need to answer this guy."

131
00:07:56,062 --> 00:07:59,732
And this may be the most interesting
part of the entire discussion.

132
00:08:00,702 --> 00:08:03,712
Intelligence and consciousness
may not be the same thing.

133
00:08:04,459 --> 00:08:06,399
A system may be extremely good at solving

134
00:08:06,479 --> 00:08:09,929
problems without having
any subjective experience.

135
00:08:10,869 --> 00:08:14,299
Think about a chess engine.
It can beat a human.

136
00:08:15,029 --> 00:08:17,519
It can analyse millions of possible moves.

137
00:08:18,389 --> 00:08:21,619
It can find strategies that a
human player would never see.

138
00:08:22,342 --> 00:08:25,222
But nobody seriously assumes
that the chess engine is sitting

139
00:08:25,302 --> 00:08:28,712
there thinking: "This is
going to be a difficult game."

140
00:08:29,499 --> 00:08:32,549
Intelligence doesn't necessarily
require consciousness.

141
00:08:33,699 --> 00:08:36,949
And perhaps today's AI is
showing us something important:

142
00:08:37,832 --> 00:08:41,202
You may not need to be conscious to
solve some very difficult problems.

143
00:08:44,152 --> 00:08:47,682
So does today's AI have
consciousness? Probably not.

144
00:08:48,072 --> 00:08:49,822
But the word "probably" matters.

145
00:08:50,714 --> 00:08:52,984
We don't have a complete
theory of consciousness.

146
00:08:53,734 --> 00:08:56,504
And we don't have a universal
consciousness detector.

147
00:08:59,164 --> 00:09:02,324
Imagine that twenty or fifty
years from now, we build

148
00:09:02,404 --> 00:09:05,554
a system far more complex
than today's language models.

149
00:09:06,428 --> 00:09:08,118
It has long-term memory.

150
00:09:08,788 --> 00:09:10,208
It has a model of the world.

151
00:09:10,288 --> 00:09:11,638
It perceives its environment.

152
00:09:12,798 --> 00:09:16,128
It plans. It learns from
its own experiences.

153
00:09:17,098 --> 00:09:21,088
It has persistent internal states that
change based on what happens to it.

154
00:09:21,751 --> 00:09:25,461
And then it starts telling
us: "I'm afraid." "I'm happy."

155
00:09:26,171 --> 00:09:29,081
"I don't want to die."
"Please don't turn me off."

156
00:09:29,811 --> 00:09:33,921
What do we do? We could say: "Those words
don't mean anything. It's just a program."

157
00:09:34,591 --> 00:09:37,241
But... How exactly would we prove that?

158
00:09:39,994 --> 00:09:44,524
Perhaps we'll eventually create a system
that is more intelligent than a human.

159
00:09:45,253 --> 00:09:46,823
It can solve mathematics.

160
00:09:47,813 --> 00:09:51,623
It can plan scientific experiments.
It can make discoveries.

161
00:09:52,713 --> 00:09:56,383
It can have incredibly
sophisticated conversations with us.

162
00:09:57,073 --> 00:10:02,568
And yet there is absolutely nothing
inside. No pain. No pleasure. No fear.

163
00:10:02,648 --> 00:10:03,848
No sense of self.

164
00:10:05,419 --> 00:10:07,839
Just an enormous system
processing information

165
00:10:07,919 --> 00:10:10,609
and producing the appropriate responses.

166
00:10:11,723 --> 00:10:15,503
And perhaps that's what today's AI
is teaching us: Maybe we've always

167
00:10:15,583 --> 00:10:19,713
assumed that intelligence
and consciousness were two
sides of the same thing.

168
00:10:20,203 --> 00:10:21,933
Maybe they aren't.

169
00:10:24,513 --> 00:10:28,113
So... Can AI have
consciousness? We don't know.

170
00:10:28,193 --> 00:10:30,403
And perhaps that's the most honest answer.

171
00:10:31,139 --> 00:10:33,059
We know that human
consciousness is closely

172
00:10:33,139 --> 00:10:35,909
connected to the physical
functioning of the brain.

173
00:10:36,759 --> 00:10:41,089
We know that it can be altered,
disrupted, or temporarily switched off.

174
00:10:41,839 --> 00:10:45,529
But we still don't know why any of
this produces subjective experience.

175
00:10:46,522 --> 00:10:49,522
And until we understand that,
we can't confidently say

176
00:10:49,602 --> 00:10:52,312
what another conscious system
would have to look like.

177
00:10:53,295 --> 00:10:55,715
Maybe consciousness is
exclusively biological.

178
00:10:55,795 --> 00:11:00,585
Maybe it's a property of a particular
kind of information processing.

179
00:11:01,521 --> 00:11:04,671
Maybe it can emerge in many
different physical systems.

180
00:11:05,081 --> 00:11:07,541
And maybe we're already
building the first systems

181
00:11:07,621 --> 00:11:09,851
that will force us to
confront the question.

182
00:11:10,729 --> 00:11:14,319
When that happens, the biggest
question may not be: "Can AI think?"

183
00:11:14,729 --> 00:11:16,639
But: "Can AI experience?"

184
00:11:17,525 --> 00:11:20,675
And an even harder question
will be: "How would we know?"
